---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Container from '../components/astro/Container.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Summary"
  description="A concise closing summary of the spec-driven enterprise context engineering presentation."
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-ink-100">
    <div class="summary-glow pointer-events-none absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <Container width="wide" class="relative py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl text-center">
        <p class="eyebrow text-accent">Part 07 · Summary</p>
        <h1
          class="display-balance mt-5 font-serif text-display-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-900 md:text-display"
        >
          The story this project is really telling.
        </h1>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600 md:text-xl">
          Hand building enterprise-ready agentic infrastructure from scratch <em
            class="font-semibold italic text-ink-900">forces</em
          > stakeholders to confront the friction between LLM behavior and enterprise constraints.
        </p>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600 md:text-xl">
          End-to-end spec-driven development <em class="font-semibold italic text-ink-900">forces</em> ALL stakeholders to
          confront the security, compliance, governance, and reliability demands of deploying AI
          inside a real business.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="py-section-sm md:py-section">
    <Container width="wide">
      <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.2fr)_minmax(18rem,0.8fr)]">
        <article class="panel p-7 md:p-9">
          <p class="eyebrow text-accent">My argument</p>
          <p class="mt-5 font-serif text-2xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink-900 md:text-3xl">
            A custom orchestration layer is not extra architecture for its own sake. It is how an
            enterprise makes its own security, reliability, and business constraints unavoidable
            parts of the AI system.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
            Without that layer, the organization inherits someone else&rsquo;s assumptions about
            what matters, which sources count, when uncertainty is acceptable, and when a human
            needs to step in. My approach treats those questions as the core product problem.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class="rounded-[1.25rem] border border-accent/20 bg-accent-soft/45 p-7 shadow-soft">
          <p class="eyebrow text-accent">What changed</p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
            The project reframes enterprise AI from &ldquo;give an agent more context&rdquo; to
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900">
              govern what context it receives, why it receives it, and what it may do next</strong
            >.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
        <article class="summary-card">
          <span class="summary-card__number">01</span>
          <h2>Research</h2>
          <p>
            The landscape showed why generic context engines, long-context prompts, and naive RAG
            are not enough by themselves. Enterprise data carries authority, permissions, freshness,
            and political ownership.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="summary-card">
          <span class="summary-card__number">02</span>
          <h2>Approach</h2>
          <p>
            The proposed answer was spec-driven enterprise context engineering: a document stack,
            hybrid source-aware retrieval, scoped context bundles, domain agents, and a
            deterministic supervisor.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="summary-card">
          <span class="summary-card__number">03</span>
          <h2>Demo</h2>
          <p>
            The replay made the architecture visible. Each step retrieved only what it needed,
            produced structured output, passed through a supervisor gate, and either continued or
            halted safely.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div
        class="mt-8 rounded-[1.5rem] border border-ink-100 bg-ink-900 p-7 text-paper shadow-soft md:p-9"
      >
        <p class="eyebrow text-paper/55">The core lesson</p>
        <div class="mt-5 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(16rem,0.6fr)]">
          <p class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold leading-snug text-paper md:text-3xl">
            The system should not feel magical. It should feel accountable.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper/70">
            A complete run is valuable because every determination is traceable. An escalation is
            valuable because ambiguity is preserved instead of hidden. The win is not just
            automation; it is a workflow that business, legal, security, procurement, and
            engineering can all inspect and trust.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl text-center">
        <p class="font-serif text-2xl leading-snug text-ink-900">
          In short: this project argues that enterprise AI becomes useful when context is not merely
          retrieved, but <strong class="font-semibold text-accent">governed</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .summary-glow {
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, rgba(157, 87, 40, 0.16), transparent 28rem),
      radial-gradient(circle at 80% 10%, rgba(33, 71, 60, 0.14), transparent 26rem);
  }

  .summary-card {
    border: 1px solid rgba(24, 33, 38, 0.08);
    border-radius: 1.25rem;
    background: rgba(252, 250, 245, 0.88);
    padding: 1.5rem;
    box-shadow:
      0 8px 24px rgba(24, 33, 38, 0.06),
      0 1px 2px rgba(24, 33, 38, 0.05);
  }

  .summary-card__number {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #9d5728;
  }

  .summary-card h2 {
    font-family: 'Iowan Old Style', 'Palatino Linotype', 'Book Antiqua', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #182126;
  }

  .summary-card p {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #47616d;
  }
</style>
